# The build context for the DEVCONTAINER image, and only for it.
#
# WHY THERE IS A SECOND ALLOWLIST AT ALL. This image used to build from
# `.devcontainer/` as its context, which was enough while everything it installed
# was a download pinned by a script sitting in that directory. It is not enough
# any more: gg's eleven program-language toolchains are pinned by the REPOSITORY —
# each arm's own `packages/gg-sandbox-*/<lang>-version.sh`, installed by
# `scripts/ci/install-gg-toolchains.sh` — and `crates/gg/build.rs` reflects all
# eleven signature catalogues as a step of building the crate, so a container that
# does not have them cannot `cargo build --workspace` at all. Baking them into the
# image therefore means the image build has to be able to READ the repository, so
# the context moved up to the repository root (see `docker-compose.yml`'s
# `context: ..`).
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS NOT THE ONLY PLACE THE SLICE IS DESCRIBED. Read the root
# `.dockerignore`'s opening paragraph: it is an allowlist rather than a list of
# exclusions precisely so "a change to anything cargo does NOT compile — the
# frontend, the docs, test cases, k8s manifests, models — can never invalidate a
# build layer". Seven Dockerfiles built from that context do `COPY . .` (the shared
# asset-tooling builder that compiles ~20 Rust binaries, the adversarial and
# performance run images, the web console image and all three service targets), so
# every path admitted there lands in seven layer cache keys. This file exists to keep
# the devcontainer's slice — in particular the narrow `packages` and `scripts/ci`
# patterns that feed a 1.9 GB layer — out of those keys, and on a Docker host it
# succeeds completely: the sibling Dockerfiles read the root allowlist, unchanged and
# unaffected.
#
# It does NOT get that for free on every host, and the paragraph below is why: the
# root allowlist now carries five `!/.devcontainer/…` entries, because a builder that
# has never heard of this file has to be able to build the image too. Those entries
# and this file are one list with two spellings — this one admits `!/.devcontainer`
# whole, the root one names the five directories the Dockerfile's COPYs read — so a
# new script directory works here and fails there. `scripts/ci/build-context.sh`
# checks this Dockerfile against BOTH allowlists for that reason.
#
# HOW DOCKER FINDS THIS FILE. BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
# beside the Dockerfile and uses it INSTEAD OF the context root's when it exists —
# so this file wins for `.devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile` and applies to nothing
# else. `docker compose build` honours the same rule, which is what matters here
# because compose is how the Dev Containers extension builds this image; verified by
# moving this file aside, at which point the compose build dies with
# `"/.devcontainer/...": not found` on the first COPY it resolves, and putting it
# back, at which point it builds.
#
# THIS REQUIRES BUILDKIT. The classic builder (`DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0`) does not know
# about per-dockerfile ignore files: it silently falls back to the root allowlist.
# Docker has defaulted to BuildKit since 23 and the devcontainer's own `docker` client
# ships `buildx`, so that is a statement of a requirement rather than a caveat. It is
# no longer a way to BREAK the build, though, because the root allowlist now admits
# this image's `.devcontainer/` sources too — see the next paragraph for why it had to.
#
# AND ROOTLESS PODMAN DOES NOT RELIABLY REACH THIS FILE — which is not hypothetical:
# `docker-compose.nixos.yml` and `.env.podman` are committed and `README.md` tells a
# NixOS user to copy them, and a rebuild on such a host is what reported it. Podman
# does not go through BuildKit. Buildah resolves an ignore file in its own order
# (`pkg/parse.ContainerIgnoreFile`): an explicit `--ignorefile`, then
# `<containerfile>.containerignore`, then `<containerfile>.dockerignore`, then
# `$CONTEXT/.containerignore`, then `$CONTEXT/.dockerignore`. So it does have a
# sibling rule, and by that order it ought to find this very file — and it did not.
# The build, driven by VS Code's Dev Containers extension through `podman-compose`,
# ended on its first COPY with
#
#     no items matching glob ".../.devcontainer/system/apt.sh" copied
#     (1 filtered out using /…/.dockerignore): no such file or directory
#
# naming the ROOT allowlist, by absolute path, as the one it applied. An older buildah
# than the sibling rule and a containerfile path `podman-compose` hands it that the
# lookup cannot join back to this directory are both consistent with that, and the two
# are not worth distinguishing, because either way the lesson is the same one: THIS
# FILE IS AN OPTIMISATION AND MAY SIMPLY NOT BE FOUND.
#
# SO THE BUILD IS MADE TO WORK IN THE ROOT ALLOWLIST, NOT HERE. It re-includes the five
# `.devcontainer/` paths this Dockerfile COPYs, and its own block there argues the
# cost (~24 files in seven `COPY . .` cache keys, against the whole of `crates/` and
# `scripts/ci` those keys already carry) and the alternative that was rejected (a root
# `.containerignore`, which Buildah prefers over `.dockerignore` for EVERY image built
# from this context, on every Podman host).
#
# So both builders now build this image, and they do not build it identically. Docker
# reads this file and gets the ~230 kB slice. Podman reads the root allowlist, and the
# difference lands on exactly ONE of the Dockerfile's COPYs: the bare
# `COPY ./packages`. Every other one names files or globs them (`install-*.sh`,
# `gg-*.sh`, `rust-toolchain.toml`), so a wider allowlist cannot widen what they take
# — but a directory copy takes whatever survives, and the root allowlist admits the
# guest packages whole (the driver image compiles them) plus `ui`, `run-record`,
# `run-stats` and the two runtimes (the web image bundles them). So on a Podman host
# that layer carries a few MB rather than the nine `*-version.sh` files and the seven
# pins named below it, and the 1.9 GB
# toolchain layer beneath it takes an unrelated `packages/ui` edit as a cache miss.
# Correct either way; cheaper under Docker. If that ever matters enough to fix, the fix
# is `podman build --ignorefile` (below), not a narrowing of the root file:
#
#     podman build --ignorefile .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile.dockerignore \
#       -f .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile -t <the tag compose expects> .
#
# `.devcontainer/README.md`'s Podman section says the same thing where a person looking
# for it would actually be standing.

# Ignore everything by default…
*

# …then re-include the four things this image's COPY instructions read.

# The devcontainer's own scripts and shell config — the whole directory, because
# every file in it exists to be copied into the image and enumerating them here
# would be a second list to keep in step with the Dockerfile's COPYs.
!/.devcontainer

# The toolchain installers, and only them, ENUMERATED. `install-gg-toolchains.sh` runs
# eight per-arm installers by that name and sources `scripts/ci/lib.sh` beside them, so
# the installers and the two named files below are the whole closure.
#
# This used to be the glob `!/scripts/ci/install-*.sh`, and the two blocks after it were
# globs too; none of the three can be. A RE-INCLUSION MUST NEVER CARRY A WILDCARD: BuildKit's
# context sender skips an ignored directory outright only while it can prove, by string
# prefix, that no negated pattern is rooted beneath it — and one `*`, `?` or `[` in any `!`
# pattern in this file turns that proof off for every directory, so the sender walks the
# whole working tree (node_modules, target/, tmp/; ~650,000 entries on a developer machine)
# to ship the few hundred kB it keeps, and one unreadable entry anywhere in that walk fails
# the build. The root allowlist records the same rule for the same reason. What the glob
# bought — a new arm's installer admitted without an edit — scripts/ci/build-context.sh now
# buys the other way round: every tracked file the three former globs matched must still
# survive this file, so a forgotten line fails CI instead of failing the devcontainer build.
#
# It is deliberately NOT the whole `scripts/ci` directory, though the root allowlist
# admits it that way for the driver image. The Dockerfile's COPY is written to match
# these same families, and the reason is the 1.9 GB layer it feeds: Docker hashes
# everything a COPY matches into that layer's cache key, so admitting the other twelve
# scripts here would mean an edit to `web-build.sh` or `specs-lint.sh` cost the next
# image build every toolchain in it.
!/scripts/ci/install-adapter.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-dotnet.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-gg-build-toolchains.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-gg-build-tools.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-gg-toolchains.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-java.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-kotlin.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-nextest.sh
# Matched by the devcontainer image's `install-*.sh` COPY, so it must be in the context too.
!/scripts/ci/install-playwright-chromium.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-purescript.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-rust-wasm.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-swift.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-uv.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-wasi-sdk.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-wit-bindgen.sh
!/scripts/ci/lib.sh
# The third file in that closure: the resuming, retrying `curl` six of those installers
# fetch their toolchain archive with. Without it they die on their own `source` line,
# having copied cleanly.
!/scripts/ci/fetch.sh

# The gg build's own shared shell, which those installers source: `gg-downloads.sh` resolves the
# pinned `wit-bindgen` and wasmtime adapter, `gg-npm-tools.sh` resolves the pinned npm-delivered
# build tools, and `gg-arms.sh` is the one list of the arms. Enumerated, for the reason above.
# Small files; the layer-cache argument above is unaffected, because these change about as
# often as an arm is added.
!/scripts/gg-arms.sh
!/scripts/gg-artifacts-out-dir.sh
!/scripts/gg-artifacts.sh
!/scripts/gg-downloads.sh
!/scripts/gg-npm-tools.sh
!/scripts/gg-probe-fixtures.sh
!/scripts/gg-reference.sh
!/scripts/gg-scratch-lock.sh
!/scripts/gg-signatures.sh

# The version files those installers source, one per arm — and nothing else under
# `packages/`: the arms' sources, their `.build/` scratch and the unrelated npm
# workspaces beside them all stay out, which is why the Dockerfile can still COPY
# `./packages` as a directory. Measured whole slice: ~230 kB. Enumerated, for the
# reason above; the gate asserts every tracked `packages/gg-sandbox-*/*-version.sh` is here.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-cpp/cpp-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-csharp/csharp-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-java/java-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-kotlin/kotlin-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/purescript-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-ruby/opal-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-ruby/yard-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/rust-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-swift/swift-version.sh

# Nine files that are NOT version files and are read by `scripts/ci/install-gg-build-tools.sh`,
# which warms the package-manager-delivered half of gg's build toolchain. They are named
# individually rather than admitted by a glob, because a glob wide enough to catch them
# (`!/packages/gg-sandbox*/build.sh`) would put ten build scripts in this layer's cache key and the
# note above `!/scripts/ci/install-*.sh` is the reason not to.
#
#   the two `build.sh`   carry the `componentize-js` and `componentize-py` pins. Those two arms have
#                        no `*-version.sh` of their own, so this is where the pin lives.
#   requirements.txt     the Python arm's wheels, pre-fetched into uv's cache.
#   the two Cargo files  the Rust arm's SEPARATE lockfile, whose registry closure nothing else in
#                        this repository warms and which that arm's build resolves `--offline`.
#   the two guest        the same for gg's ECMAScript guest, a crate outside the workspace with a
#   Cargo files          lockfile of its own, built `--locked --offline` by its `guest.sh`. Only the
#                        manifest and the lockfile: the manifest names its `[lib] path` so
#                        `cargo fetch` does not look for `src/`, which keeps the guest's sources out
#                        of this layer's cache key.
#   spago.yaml           read by TWO things, and the first is why it cannot wait for the glob above:
#                        `purescript-version.sh` does not merely sit beside it, it READS it. The
#                        registry package set is written down once, where Spago reads it, and that
#                        version file projects it with a `sed` that `exit 1`s on an empty result —
#                        so every installer that sources the PureScript pin fails inside the image
#                        without this line. The second reader is the build-tool installer, below.
#   spago.lock           `install-gg-build-tools.sh` warms Spago's package STORE by running `spago
#                        install` in that package, and the lockfile decides which versions it
#                        resolves to. A warm without it caches a set the arm's build re-resolves,
#                        which is worse than no warm because it looks like it worked.
!/packages/gg-sandbox/build.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-python/build.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-python/requirements.txt
!/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/Cargo.toml
!/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/Cargo.lock
!/packages/gg-sandbox/guest/Cargo.toml
!/packages/gg-sandbox/guest/Cargo.lock
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.yaml
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.lock

# `packages/gg-sandbox-rust/rust-version.sh` reads the toolchain channel out of
# this file rather than pinning a second copy of it, so the Rust arm's installer
# (`rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`) cannot run without it. It is also
# what makes that install cheap: `channel` here equals the `RUST_VERSION` build arg
# in `docker-compose.yml`, so rustup adds a target to the toolchain the image
# already has rather than downloading a second one.
!/rust-toolchain.toml

# Finally, re-exclude the two host-specific files that are deliberately not
# committed (see `.devcontainer/.gitignore`). They would ride in under the
# `!/.devcontainer` re-inclusion above, which would put one developer's SSH-agent
# path and timezone into the context of everyone's build. Nothing COPYs them —
# `docker-compose.local.yml` is read by compose on the HOST, before any of this.
# These come last so they win.
/.devcontainer/docker-compose.local.yml
/.devcontainer/.env

# And macOS Finder metadata, which rides in under the same `!/.devcontainer`
# re-inclusion for the same reason and is worse: those two files at least only change
# when a developer edits them, while a `.DS_Store` is rewritten because a window was
# resized or a folder scrolled — churn with no edit behind it at all. Every rewrite
# changes this image's `COPY` cache key, so a devcontainer rebuild re-runs the
# toolchain installs it would otherwise have skipped. The primary development host is
# macOS sharing this tree into a Linux VM, so Finder and the builder see the same
# directories. The root allowlist carries the same entry; this is the half of that
# pair that scripts/ci/build-context.sh checks against THIS file.
**/.DS_Store
